        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Health and fitness publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends almost entirely on the credential behind it.</p>

            <p>The medically-reviewed health brands do not take open guest posts. The fitness magazines take certified contributors. The nutrition outlets want qualified authors. The coaching blogs are open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Medically-reviewed health brands</td><td>Healthline, WebMD, Verywell, Everyday Health, Harvard Health</td><td>Not open guest posts. A byline from a physician, RD or licensed professional</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Fitness &amp; training magazines</td><td>Men's Health, Women's Health, Shape, Runner's World, Muscle &amp; Strength, BarBend</td><td>Certified contributors; programming, technique, training science with citations</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Nutrition &amp; wellness</td><td>Precision Nutrition, Examine, Diet Doctor, MindBodyGreen, Wellness Mama</td><td>Qualified authors; evidence-based nutrition, protocols, myth-busting</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Coaching &amp; training blogs</td><td>Nerd Fitness, Stronger by Science, Stronglifts, Girls Gone Strong, BoxRox</td><td>Open to contributions; programs, coaching lessons, first-hand results with data</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> health and fitness is core YMYL. Google's systems weigh named, credentialed authors and medical review heavily, so a link earned with a real expert byline is in a different class from a link on a site full of anonymous posts.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 health &amp; fitness sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and redirected sites were swapped for live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
