                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for health editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the credential and the evidence.</strong> "As a registered dietitian, here is what the latest protein research actually shows" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Cite primary sources.</strong> Link the studies, not a summary of a summary. Health editors check.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your strength-training coverage" or "for the nutrition desk" shows you read the publication.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> Health brands want a reviewed explainer. Fitness magazines want programming with technique cues. Coaching blogs want a real program and real results.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring the assets.</strong> A protocol, a data set, progress photos with context, a demonstration video.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any product, clinic or program</strong> you have a stake in.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it is an ad for your program, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a "10 best exercises" listicle with no author credential.</li>
              <li>Make health or supplement claims you cannot back with a study.</li>
              <li>Send a nutrition piece with no RD, MD or named expert attached.</li>
              <li>Put exact-match anchors ("best pre-workout") on links back to your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated health content. In a YMYL niche it is both obvious and risky.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on medically-reviewed sites with a real expert byline will do more for rankings and trust than a long list of thin fitness-blog links. Use this list to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

